"Internal Error ()\nciteturn113view0 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://pubs.rsc.org/en/content/articlelanding/2015/ra/c4ra15451g\",\"lineno\":null}); \nFailed to fetch https://pubs.rsc.org/en/content/articlelanding/2015/ra/c4ra15451g: (403) Forbidden--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nProgrammable motion of DNA origami mechanisms - PMC (https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4311804/)\nciteturn113view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4311804/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 386\nL0: 【0†Skip to main content】\nL1: \nL2: 【1†Image†cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov】 Search\nL3: \nL4: 【2†Image†cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov】 Log in 【3†Image†cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov】 【4†Image†cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov】\nL5: \nL6:   * 【5†Dashboard†www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov】\nL7:   * 【6†Publications†www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov】\nL8:   * 【7†Account settings†www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov】\nL9:   * [Button: Log out ]\nL10: \nL11: Search… [Input: Search NCBI] Search NCBI\nL12: \nL13: Primary site navigation\nL14: \nL15: 【8†Image: Close†cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov】\nL16: \nL17: Search [Input: Search NCBI]\nL18: \nL19: Logged in as:\nL20:   * 【5†Dashboard†www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov】\nL21:   * 【6†Publications†www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov】\nL22:   * 【7†Account settings†www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov】\nL23: \nL24: [Button: Log in ]\nL25: \nL26: 【1†Image: PMC search open icon†cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov】 【8†Image: PMC search close ison†cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov】\nL27: \nL28: Search PMC Full-Text Archive [Input: Search PMC Full-Text Archive] Search in PMC 【9†Image: Search†cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov】\nL29: \nL30:   * 【10†Journal List】\nL31:   * 【11†User Guide】\nL32: \nL33: [Button: Proc Natl Acad Sci U S A]\nL34: . 2015 Jan 5;112(3):713–718. doi: 【12†10.1073/pnas.1408869112†doi.org】\nL35: # Programmable motion of DNA origami mechanisms\nL36: \nL37: 【13†Alexander E Marras†pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov】\nL38: \nL39: ### Alexander E Marras\nL40: \nL41: ^{1}Department of Mechanical and Aerospace Engineering, The Ohio State University, Columbus, OH 43210\nL42: \nL43: Find articles by 【13†Alexander E Marras†pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov】\nL44: \nL45: ^{1}, 【14†Lifeng Zhou†pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov】\nL46: ### Lifeng Zhou\nL47: \nL48: ^{1}Department of Mechanical and Aerospace Engineering, The Ohio State University, Columbus, OH 43210\nL49: \nL50: Find articles by 【14†Lifeng Zhou†pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov】\nL51: \nL52: ^{1}, 【15†Hai-Jun Su†pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov】\nL53: \nL54: ### Hai-Jun Su\nL55: \nL56: ^{1}Department of Mechanical and Aerospace Engineering, The Ohio State University, Columbus, OH 43210\nL57: \nL58: Find articles by 【15†Hai-Jun Su†pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov】\nL59: \nL60: ^{1}, 【16†Carlos E Castro†pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov】\nL61: ### Carlos E Castro\nL62: \nL63: ^{1}Department of Mechanical and Aerospace Engineering, The Ohio State University, Columbus, OH 43210\nL64: \nL65: Find articles by 【16†Carlos E Castro†pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov】\nL66: \nL67: ^{1,}^{1}\nL68: \nL69:   * [Button: Author information]\nL70:   * [Button: Article notes]\nL71:   * [Button: Copyright and License information]\nL72: \nL73: ^{1}Department of Mechanical and Aerospace Engineering, The Ohio State University, Columbus, OH 43210\nL74: \nL75: ^{1}\nL76: \nL77: To whom correspondence should be addressed. Email: castro.39@osu.edu.\nL78: Edited by William Shih, Harvard University, Cambridge, MA, and accepted by the Editorial Board December 10, 2014 (received for review May 13, 2014)\nL79: \nL80: Author contributions: A.E.M., L.Z., H.-J.S., and C.E.C. designed research; A.E.M. and L.Z. performed research; A.E.M., H.-J.S., and C.E.C. analyzed data; and A.E.M. and C.E.C. wrote the paper.\nL81: \nL82: Issue date 2015 Jan 20.\nL83: \nL84: 【17†PMC Copyright notice】\nL85: \nL86: PMCID: PMC4311804  PMID: 【18†25561550†pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov】\nL87: ## Significance\nL88: Folding DNA into complex 3D shapes (DNA origami) has emerged as a powerful method for the precise design and fabrication of self-assembled nanodevices. Current efforts have focused largely on developing static objects or structures with small movements and/or unspecified motion paths. Here we establish a basis for developing DNA-based nanomachines by creating dynamic mechanisms with well-defined motion.\nL89: We demonstrate the design of nanoscale 1D, 2D, and 3D motion by integrating concepts from engineering machine design with DNA origami nanotechnology.\nL90: Keywords: DNA nanotechnology, DNA origami, dynamic structures, machine design, self-assembly\nL91: ## Abstract\nL92: DNA origami enables the precise fabrication of nanoscale geometries. We demonstrate an approach to engineer complex and reversible motion of nanoscale DNA origami machine elements.\nL93: We first design, fabricate, and characterize the mechanical behavior of flexible DNA origami rotational and linear joints that integrate stiff double-stranded DNA components and flexible single-stranded DNA components to constrain motion along a single degree of freedom and demonstrate the ability to tune the flexibility and range of motion. Multiple joints with simple 1D motion were then integrated into higher order mechanisms.\nL94: One mechanism is a crank–slider that couples rotational and linear motion, and the other is a Bennett linkage that moves between a compacted bundle and an expanded frame configuration with a constrained 3D motion path. Finally, we demonstrate distributed actuation of the linkage using DNA input strands to achieve reversible conformational changes of the entire structure on ∼minute timescales.\nL95: Our results demonstrate programmable motion of 2D and 3D DNA origami mechanisms constructed following a macroscopic machine design approach.\nL96: * * *\nL97: The ability to control, manipulate, and organize matter at the nanoscale has demonstrated immense potential for advancements in industrial technology, medicine, and materials (【19†1】–【20†3】). Bottom-up self-assembly has become a particularly promising area for nanofabrication (【21†4】, 【22†5】); however, to date designing complex motion at the nanoscale remains a challenge (【23†6】–【24†9】).\nL98: Amino acid polymers exhibit well-defined and complex dynamics in natural systems and have been assembled into designed structures including nanotubes, sheets, and networks (【25†10】–【26†12】), although the complexity of interactions that govern amino acid folding make designing complex geometries extremely challenging. DNA nanotechnology, on the other hand, has exploited well-understood assembly properties of DNA to create a variety of increasingly complex designed nanostructures (【27†13】–【28†15】).\nL99: Scaffolded DNA origami, the process of folding a long single-stranded DNA (ssDNA) strand into a custom structure (【29†16】–【30†18】), has enabled the fabrication of nanoscale objects with unprecedented geometric complexity that have recently been implemented in applications such as containers for drug delivery (【31†19】, 【32†20】), nanopores for single-molecule sensing (【33†21】–【34†23】), and templates for nanoparticles (【35†24】, 【36†25】) or proteins (【37†26】–【38†28】).\nL100: The majority of these and other applications of DNA origami have largely focused on static structures. Natural biomolecular machines, in contrast, have a rich diversity of functionalities that rely on complex but well-defined and reversible conformational changes. Currently, the scope of biomolecular nanotechnology is limited by an inability to achieve similar motion in designed nanosystems.\nL101: DNA nanotechnology has enabled critical steps toward that goal starting with the work of Mao et al. (【39†29】), who developed a DNA nanostructure that took advantage of the B–Z transition of DNA to switch states. Since then, efforts to fabricate dynamic DNA systems have primarily focused on strand displacement approaches (【40†30】) mainly on systems comprising a few strands or arrays of strands undergoing ∼nm-scale motions (【41†31】–【42†37】) in some cases guided by DNA origami templates (【43†38】–【44†40】).\nL102: More recently, strand displacement has been used to reconfigure DNA origami nanostructures, for example opening DNA containers (【31†19】, 【45†41】, 【46†42】), controlling molecular binding (【47†43】, 【48†44】), or reconfiguring structures (【49†45】). The largest triggerable structural change was achieved by Han et al. in a DNA origami Möbius strip (one-sided ribbon structure) that could be opened to approximately double in size (【49†45】).\nL103: Constrained motion has been achieved in systems with rotational motion (【31†19】, 【32†20】, 【50†32】, 【45†41】, 【48†44】, 【51†46】, 【52†47】) in some cases to open lid-like components (【31†19】, 【32†20】, 【45†41】) or detect molecular binding (【48†44】, 【53†48】, 【54†49】). A few of these systems achieved reversible conformational changes (【50†32】, 【45†41】, 【48†44】, 【51†46】), although the motion path and flexibility were not studied. Constrained linear motion has remained largely unexplored.\nL104: Linear displacements on the scale of a few nanometers have been demonstrated via conformational changes of DNA structure motifs (【55†50】–【56†55】), strand invasion to open DNA hairpins (【57†36】, 【56†55】, 【58†56】), or the reversible sliding motion of a DNA tile actuator (【58†56】); these cases also did not investigate the motion path or flexibility of motion.\nL105: Building on these prior studies, this work implements concepts from macroscopic machine design to build modular parts with constrained motion. We demonstrate an ability to tune the flexibility and range of motion and then integrate these parts into prototype mechanisms with designed 2D and 3D motion. We further demonstrate reversible actuation of a mechanism with complex conformational changes on minute timescales.\nL106: ## DNA Origami Mechanism Design\nL107: A kinematic mechanism is a mechanical device that transforms an input motion from an actuator to a defined motion pattern (【59†57】). They typically comprise a collection of interconnected components called links whose motion is constrained by so-called kinematic joints, such as revolute (hinge), prismatic (slider), and spherical joints. Complex 3D motion is achieved by integrating multiple joints with precisely designed geometric components into a mechanism.\nL108: In nanoscale design, specific mechanical motion has seldom been explored. Whereas rotational (【31†19】, 【32†20】, 【50†32】, 【45†41】, 【48†44】, 【51†46】–【54†49】) and small linear motions (【57†36】, 【55†50】–【58†56】) have been demonstrated, the mechanics of this motion have not been studied in any detail. Here we use scaffolded DNA origami to integrate stiff double-stranded DNA (dsDNA) and flexible ssDNA components to create mechanical devices capable of precise motion over ∼10–100-nm range.\nL109: Our design approach, which parallels macroscopic mechanism design, is conceptualized in 【60†Fig. 1】 with an example of a mechanism that couples rotational and linear motion. In macroscopic machines, joints are typically designed by combining stiff components along an edge (e.g., hinge; 【60†Fig. 1】, Top) or with complementary geometry (e.g., slider; 【60†Fig. 1】, Middle).\nL110: In DNA origami, similar functionality can be achieved either by strategic placement of flexible ssDNA connections (e.g., along a line to form a hinge axis) or complementary geometry (e.g., concentric structures). These joints can be combined to create higher order mechanisms (e.g., crank–slider; 【60†Fig. 1】, Bottom) with complex predefined motion. We focus on slider and hinge joints and their implementation into mechanisms with controllable motion.\nL111: ### Fig. 1.\nL112: \nL113: 【61†Image: Fig. 1.†cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov】\nL114: \nL115: 【62†Open in a new tab】\nL116: DNA origami mechanism design. Our approach to DNA origami mechanism design follows macroscopic machine design starting with isolated joints for angular (Top) or linear (Middle) motion. Joints can be integrated to achieve complex motion as shown here for a crank–slider mechanism (Bottom). Left shows macroscale solid models and Right shows their DNA origami counterparts. In the DNA origami designs, cylinders represent dsDNA helices.\nL117: ## DNA Nanostructures for Simple Motion\nL118: A DNA origami slider joint was created to produce linear motion by connecting two stiff bundles with complementary geometry using flexible ssDNA scaffold connections as depicted in 【63†Fig. 2】. The two components are self-assembled concentrically, which to our knowledge has not previously been shown with DNA origami. The outer tube of 30 helices surrounds an inner 6-helix bundle (hb) with roughly 2 nm of clearance.\nL119: At the base of the 6 hb is a 42 hb, which sterically limits the translation of the outer tube. Long ssDNA scaffold strands connect the outer tube and inner 6 hb to allow for relative linear motion (【63†Fig. 2A】).\nL120: ### Fig. 2.\nL121: \nL122: 【64†Open in a new tab】\nL123: DNA origami sliders. (A) The slider consists of two stiff components folded concentrically and connected only with ssDNA scaffold facilitating linear motion. Two versions of the slider were fabricated, one with short ssDNA connections (version 1) and one with long ssDNA connections (version 2). TEM images illustrate different conformations of version 2. Scale bar, 50 nm (B) Version 1 (shorter connections) is shown via TEM.\nL124: Scale bar, 100 nm (C) A linear distribution of 14 nm was measured from 275 samples of version 1. (D) A wider linear distribution was measured from 251 samples of version 2. (E) The energy landscape was calculated for both versions from the linear distributions assuming Boltzmann energy weighting (scale bar indicates an energy scale of k_{B}T). The lines show cubic spline fits to linear distributions.\nL125: (F) The energy landscape was differentiated to determine the force required to hold each joint at any specific length.\nL126: We fabricated two versions of the slider (【63†Fig. 2 C and D】) with different ranges of motion and stiffness controlled by varying the length of the ssDNA scaffold connections. Version 1 has six 49-nucleotide (nt) connections between the 30-hb tube and 42-hb base and six 28-nt connections between the 30-hb tube and inner 6 hb at the opposite end. Six 15-nt staples were added to prevent hairpins in the 49-nt connections (【65†Fig. S1】).\nL127: Thermal fluctuations cause the joint to extend and contract along the linear motion path. The extensional distribution (【63†Fig. 2B】), measured by transmission electron microscopy (TEM), revealed a range of thermal motion of 14.0 nm. The second version, with a larger range of motion, has 86-nt connections between the tube and base and 96-nt connections between the tube and the end of the 6 hb. Three 18–20-nt staples were added to each connection to prevent hairpins (【65†Fig. S1】).\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nChecking your browser - reCAPTCHA (https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4288979/)\nciteturn113view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4288979/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 3\nL0: Checking your browser before accessing pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov ...\nL1: \nL2: Click 【0†here】 if you are not automatically redirected after 5 seconds.\n"
